{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And by the breath of Your nostrils.</b> That which comes out of the two nostrils of the nose. Scripture speaks, as it were, in the manner of the Shechinah patterned after a king of flesh and blood, in order to make the ear of creatures hear according to what is — that they should be able to understand the matter; when a person is angry, breath goes out from his nostrils. And thus: ״עָלָה עָשָׁן בְּאַפּוֹ״ (Psalms 18), and thus ״וּמֵרוּחַ אַפּוֹ יִכְלוּ״ (Job 4), and this is what is said: ״לְמַעַן שְׁמִי אַאֲרִיךְ אַפִּי״ (Isaiah 48) — when His anger rests, His breathing is long, and when He is angry, His breathing is short; ״וּתְהִלָּתִי אֶחֱטָם לָךְ״ (ibid.) — and for the sake of My praise I will set a plug (חֹטֶם) in My nose to stop up My nostrils against the nose and the breath, that they should not go out; לָךְ — for your sake; אֶחֱטָם, like ״נְאָקָה בְּחֹטֶם״ in tractate Shabbat — so it appears to me. And every expression of חֲרוֹן אַף in Scripture I say thus: חָרָה אַף is like ״וְעַצְמִי חָרָה מִנִּי חֹרֶב״ (Job 30) — a term of burning and kindling, for the nostrils grow hot and glow at the time of rage; and חָרוֹן is derived from חָרָה, just as רָצוֹן is from רָצָה; and so חֵמָה is a term of heat, therefore it says ״וַחֲמָתוֹ בָּעֲרָה בוֹ״ (Esther 1); and when the חֵמָה rests, it says ״נִתְקָרְרָה״ — his mind cooled." "<b>The waters were piled up.</b> Onkelos rendered it as a term of piling (עַרְמִימוּת), and the plain sense of Scripture is like ״עֲרֵמַת חִטִּים״ (Song of Songs 7), and ״נִצְּבוּ כְמוֹ נֵד״ proves it. <b>Were piled up.</b> From the searing wind that went out from Your nostrils the waters dried up, and they became like heaps and mounds of a pile, high ones." "<b>Like a wall.</b> As its Targum [renders]: ״כְּשׁוּר״ — like a wall." "<b>Wall.</b> A term of heaping and gathering, like ״נֵד קָצִיר בְּיוֹם נַחֲלָה״ (Isaiah 17:11), ״כוֹנֵס כַּנֵּד״ (Psalms 33:7). It is not written כוֹנֵס כַּנֹּאד but כַּנֵּד; and if כנד were like כנאד and כוֹנֵס a term of bringing in, it should have written מַכְנִיס כִּבְנֹאד מֵי הַיָּם. Rather, כוֹנֵס is a term of gathering and heaping. And thus: ״קָמוּ נֵד אֶחָד״ (Joshua 3:13), ״וַיַּעַמְדוּ נֵד אֶחָד״ (ibid.) — and there is no term of rising and standing for wineskins, but only for walls and heaps. And we do not find נֹאד punctuated [with a holeam] except in its full [plene] form, like ״שִׂימָה דִמְעָתִי בְנֹאדֶךָ״ (Psalms 56:9), ״אֶת נֹאד הֶחָלָב״ (Judges 4:19)." "<b>Congealed.</b> Like ״וְכַגְּבִנָּה תַּקְפִּיאֵנִי״ (Job 10:10) — they hardened and became like stones, and the waters hurled the Egyptians against the rock with force and fought against them with all kinds of harshness." "<b>בלב ים.</b> In the strength of the sea. And it is the way of Scripture to speak thus: ״עַד לֵב הַשָּׁמַיִם״ (Deuteronomy 4), ״בְּלֵב הָאֵלָה״ (II Samuel 18) — a term for the core and the strength of a thing."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "Rashi reads אֶחֱטָם as 'I will plug' (from חָטַם, to stop up), punning on חֹטֶם, 'nose'; the rendering preserves his reading of the Isaiah verse not as the usual 'I will defer My anger' but as 'I will plug [My] nose.'", 3 "Rashi's argument turns on vowel points: נֵד (segol) = 'heap/wall,' נֹאד (holeam) = 'wineskin'; in כַּנֵּד the vowel is segol, proving it means heap, not skin."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:52:40.277223495Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}